etected in the serum of the infant, although the weakly active metabolite norsertraline (desmethylsertraline) is often detectable in low levels in infant serum. Rarely, preterm infants with impaired metabolic activity might accumulate the drug and demonstrate symptoms similar to neonatal abstinence. No adverse effects on development have been found in infants followed for up to 5 years of age. Most authoritative reviewers consider sertraline a preferred antidepressants during breastfeeding.[1-9] Mothers taking an SSRI during pregnancy and postpartum may have more difficulty breastfeeding, although this might be a reflection of their disease state.[10] These mothers may need additional breastfeeding support. Breastfed infants exposed to an SSRI during the third trimester of pregnancy are at risk for neonatal withdrawal syndrome consisting of hypoglycemia, tremors, tachycardia, or hypotonia, and might be at a greater risk of readmission due to jaundice.[11,12]","truncated":false,"source_section":"Summary of Use during Lactation","notice":"Preserve relevant source context when describing adverse findings; this is source text, not a personal treatment plan."},"alternatives_policy":{"handling":"excluded","present_in_parsed_source":true,"notice":"Alternate Drugs to Consider is excluded from returned text and synthesis. Consult the linked LactMed source; this tool does not recommend substitutions."},"population_context":{"source_population":"lactation","pregnancy_evidence":false},"continuity_of_care_notice":"Stopping or changing a medication can also carry risks. Discuss changes with the prescribing clinician. This tool cannot advise starting, stopping, or changing treatment.","section_population_context":{"Summary of Use during Lactation":{"requested_population":null,"source_population":"medicine_taker_label_population","source_basis":"Explicit source section; confirm the exact product and actual population.","matches_requested_population_confirmed":false,"age_band_confir